@startuml window_memory_restore
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' window_memory_restore — v23/v23.1, restoring and saving the window.
' Converted from Mermaid in the v26.8 audit (never rendered until now).
'
' FOUR ways in, ONE way out: every failure path lands on the same default
' rectangle, so no branch needs its own recovery code. That is the shape
' worth stealing — an error-handling design where the failures converge
' instead of each growing a bespoke tail.
'
' The interesting check is the LAST one, because Qt cannot do it for you:
' restoreGeometry() validates BYTES, not monitors. A perfectly valid blob can
' put your window on a screen that went home with the docking station.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!include _style.puml

title The window's memory — restore (four ways in, one way out) and save

start
:MainWindow constructor ends;
:<b>resize(kDefaultWidth, kDefaultHeight)</b>
set FIRST, so every exit below
already stands on a sane window;
:restoreWindowState();
:RAIL — m_nav->setVisible(
  prefs::sidebarVisible(!isCompactScreen()));

if (isCompactScreen()?) then (yes)
  :<b>DONE</b> — geometry is neither saved
  nor restored on phone-sized screens;
  stop
else (no)
endif

if (prefs::windowGeometry() empty?) then (yes — first run here)
  :<b>DONE</b> — default rectangle;
  stop
else (no)
endif

if (restoreGeometry(blob)) then (false — Qt refused the bytes:\nwrong version, corrupt, hand-edited)
  :<b>DONE</b> — default rectangle;
  stop
else (true)
endif

if (overlapsAnyScreen(frameGeometry(),\navailableScreenRects())) then (true)
  :<b>DONE</b> — window is where you left it,
  maximized state and all;
  stop
else (false — the monitor it lived on is gone)
  :setWindowState(WindowNoState)
  — drop a maximize aimed at a screen
  that no longer exists;
  :resize + centre on primary;
  :<b>DONE</b> — default rectangle.
  Silent: an unreadable preference is
  not an error the user can act on;
  stop
endif

@enduml
